/**
 * Chemistry described qualitatively on purpose. No specific temperature
 * thresholds, no "receipts fade in N weeks" figures — those vary by paper
 * stock and conditions and would be invented precision.
 */
---

<Article
  slug="why-thermal-receipts-fade"
  toolLine="Wondering what the fading has already cost you? Four questions, no signup, no email."
>
  <p class="lede">
    There is no ink on a fuel receipt. That single fact explains everything that
    goes wrong with it afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2>How thermal paper actually works</h2>

  <p>
    A thermal printer has no ribbon, no toner, and no ink cartridge. What it has
    is a row of tiny heating elements and a special paper stock coated with a
    chemical layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    That coating contains two key components held apart from each other: a
    colourless dye compound and an acidic developer. Apply heat and the layer
    softens enough for the two to meet and react, and the dye turns dark. That
    reaction is your receipt. The printer is not adding anything to the paper
    &mdash; it is triggering a reaction in a coating that was already there.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why thermal printing is so well suited to fuel pumps and truck
    stops. There is nothing to replace but the paper roll, the mechanism is
    simple, and it works reliably in a machine that lives outdoors.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is also why the receipt is not permanent.
  </p>

  <h2>The same mechanism that prints it destroys it</h2>

  <p>
    If heat is what causes the reaction, then heat is also what the finished
    receipt is vulnerable to. Warm the whole sheet and you are effectively
    running the printing process across all of it at once &mdash; or degrading
    the coating so the contrast between marked and unmarked areas collapses.
    Either way the outcome is the same: nothing readable.
  </p>

  <p>
    A parked truck in summer is close to the worst environment you could design
    for this. A closed cab in direct sun gets substantially hotter than the air
    outside, and it stays that way for hours at a stretch, day after day. A
    receipt on the dash or in the door pocket is being slowly cooked for weeks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sunlight adds a second, independent problem. Ultraviolet light breaks down
    the dye compound directly, so a receipt in the light is being attacked from
    two directions at once.
  </p>

  <h2>The failure modes nobody expects</h2>

  <p>
    Heat and light are the obvious culprits. There are others that catch people
    out because they seem harmless:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Plastics.</strong> Many soft plastics contain plasticisers that
      migrate into whatever they touch, and they interfere with the coating.
      Storing receipts in a plastic sleeve or a vinyl document wallet &mdash;
      which feels like protecting them &mdash; can be actively worse than a
      paper envelope.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Oils and solvents.</strong> Diesel, hand cleaner, grease, and
      hand sanitiser all attack the coating. Handling a receipt at a fuel island
      with dirty hands is not a neutral act.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Friction.</strong> The coating sits on the surface. Rubbing wears
      it, which is why a receipt loose in a pocket for a week is often smeared
      even if it has been nowhere near heat.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Other thermal paper.</strong> Stacked face-to-face over time,
      receipts can transfer chemicals between sheets and mark each other.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Notice how many of these are consequences of ordinary careful storage. The
    plastic wallet in the glovebox is a genuinely well-intentioned solution that
    makes the problem worse.
  </p>

  <h2>How long you have</h2>

  <p>
    Honestly: it depends, and anyone who gives you a specific number is guessing.
    Paper stocks differ, coatings differ, and conditions differ enormously
    between a receipt in a cool cab in October and one on a dashboard in Texas
    in July.
  </p>

  <p>
    What can be said without inventing precision is the direction of travel. The
    degradation starts immediately and never reverses. There is no point at
    which a fading receipt stabilises, and there is no way to restore one once
    the text has gone. Recovery is not a service that exists.
  </p>

  <p>
    The practical consequence is that the value of a thermal receipt is highest
    the moment it is printed and declines from there. Which inverts the usual
    instinct about paperwork, where the sensible move is to deal with it later
    when you have time.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually works</h2>

  <p>
    You cannot stop the chemistry. What you can do is take a copy while the
    original is still readable.
  </p>

  <p>
    A photograph does not fade. Once the image exists, the thermal receipt can
    do whatever it likes &mdash; the record has already been made, at the moment
    the paper was at its most legible. That is the entire logic behind capturing
    at the pump rather than filing at home.
  </p>

  <p>
    It also reframes the storage question. If the photo is the record, then the
    glovebox stops being a filing system and goes back to being what it is good
    at: holding things you have already dealt with. No plastic wallet required.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>One thing worth checking:</strong> if you are relying on
      photographs, confirm early that they are genuinely readable rather than
      assuming. A capture habit producing blurry images feels like it is working
      right up until the moment you need one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For what makes a record adequate in the first place, see
    <a href="/guide/irs-receipt-requirements">IRS receipt requirements</a>. For
    which slips are worth the three seconds, see
    <a href="/blog/what-receipts-to-keep-trucking">what receipts to keep</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
